MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE MECHANISM, settled from source on browser#131 and not re-derived here. The
batch weave input is ONE window-sized texture; each target's side-by-side is
copied into it AT ITS OWN WINDOW POSITION and the input is never cleared, and
the copy order is the aggregator's base::Token sort, which has nothing to do
with z. The runtime then samples each submitted rect's LEFT half into the left
eye and its RIGHT half into the right eye, so an overlap that falls in one half
of a rect corrupts exactly ONE EYE: the lower tile keeps a clean left eye and
grows a displaced replica of its neighbour in the right. (The competing
candidate, a stale |drawn_rect|, was REFUTED: the field has had no reader on
this arm since 0046 deleted the per-tile draw-back.)

Clipping the COPY cannot fix it -- both tiles need their own pixels at the same
input address. Disjointness has to be achieved at the RECT level.

THE POLICY (David, decided on browser#131): TOPMOST-WINS. In an overlap the
UPPER tile owns the pixels -- that is what the DOM already says, and it is the
only answer that keeps depth for the tile the author put in front. The LOWER
tile's weave rect is reduced by the upper tile's rect.

1. CARRY Z-ORDER TO THE GPU STAGE. Until now "lower" was not nameable there.
   The aggregator already knows the paint order: |root_resource_quads_| is
   filled during CopyQuadsToPass, which walks each pass's quad_list in its
   natural FRONT-TO-BACK order (the same convention
   ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit's |k| uses, and the reverse of the BackToFront
   replay). Stamp that recording index onto each RootResourceQuad, read it
   through the EXISTING identity join, and carry it on
   AggregatedFrame::Inline3dTrackedRect -> SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dRect ->
   the GPU stage. Smaller == upper. An element no root quad claims (browser#94
   offscreen tile, legacy channel) keeps kUnknownZOrder and sorts bottom-most;
   the vector position breaks ties so the relation is total and stable.

2. SUBTRACT, DON'T CLOBBER. Before the submit loop, reduce each target's rect
   by the rect of every target above it. The reduction is a ROW BAND and never
   a column band, and that is not a shortcut -- a submitted rect is not a
   window onto the input, it is a SIDE-BY-SIDE PAIR whose eye split is the
   rect's own horizontal midpoint. Trimming rows leaves the split where it was;
   trimming columns MOVES it, so the runtime would cut the pair somewhere other
   than between the eyes and hand each eye a mixture of both -- re-creating, by
   construction, the exact eye-inconsistency this patch ends. So:

     * SUBTRACT (exact) -- the overlap spans the lower tile's full width and
       touches its top or bottom edge. The remainder is one rect; the lower
       tile weaves everything it still owns in stereo and the upper weaves the
       overlap in stereo. Nothing is lost.
     * FALLBACK (tier-2, the documented one) -- every other geometry: a corner
       overlap (the case reported), an interior one, or any overlap narrower
       than the tile. Keep the LARGER clean row band and WITHHOLD the rest.

   |rect| itself is deliberately NOT reduced: the scratch stays sized to and
   composed at the WHOLE tile, so the withheld rows keep a live mono source and
   a permanently-overlapped tile never freezes. Only the handoff is cropped --
   a source sub-rect for WeaveCanvas / WeaveCanvasMac, a whole-scanline pointer
   offset for WeavePixels, and |weave| for WeaveRectAndroid and pending_rect.
   Every one of them is an identity when nothing overlaps.

3. DRAW THE WITHHELD ROWS MONO, via 0112's primitive. Those rows are woven by
   nobody and their canvas quad is suppressed from the page raster, so without
   this they are the browser#99 hole: black. The recovery loop's
   "already submitted, don't repaint" rule is right about the band that wove
   and wrong about everything else the tile owns, so the mono draw is clipped
   to the withheld rows -- it cannot reach the woven band. It also falls out of
   the wish (wish = union(weave rects) - union(flat rects)), so the panel's
   lens switches OFF over those rows and the flat pixels stay crisp.

4. NEVER KEEP-PREVIOUS AN OVERLAPPING TILE. |batch_last_copied_| records
   "target P's pixels are at rect P", and where two tiles shared input pixels
   that record can name a region the other tile has since written -- a replay
   would re-weave the neighbour's content and log it as a clean keep. Both ends
   of an overlapping pair decline and take the mono road instead.

THE NON-OVERLAPPING CASE IS BYTE-IDENTICAL. With no overlap the computed band
is bit-for-bit the rect the pre-patch code produced, the source sub-rects and
pointer offsets degenerate to their previous values, keep-previous is
untouched, and the withheld region is empty so the mono leg never runs. The
only cost is one O(N^2) rect-intersect over a handful of tiles.

Overlaps are never silent: one throttled line (first 3, then 1-in-300) names
each pair, its z values, the overlap, the kept band, and whether it took
SUBTRACT or FALLBACK; the recovery line gains a withheld= count.
